      heading: "Ten questions to ask before you subscribe",
      body:
        "Before any fresh dog food subscription starts, ten questions - most answerable from the brand's own pages in one evening - turn a hopeful purchase into an informed one. One: is every recipe I would feed complete and balanced per its AAFCO statement, for my dog's life stage - with large-breed growth language if I am raising one? Two: who formulates the recipes, and will the brand share full nutritional analyses? Named credentials and frictionless data are the serious answers. Three: what is the real per-day price for my dog - full-fresh and mixed - from the quote flow with honest inputs? Your number; the only number. Four: what does the trial actually include, what does the steady state cost after, and what happens if it does not work out? Five: what formats exist - fresh only, or baked or dry lines that blend - and does my freezer, travel pattern and budget want that flexibility? The formats question sorts this category more than any other. Six: how do pause, skip, resize and cancel actually work? Read the mechanics; the freezer-overflow month happens to everyone. Seven: how does food arrive, and what is the stated policy when a box shows up warm? The cold chain is the product; the policy says whether the brand believes it. Eight: what does the brand's verified public review record look like - score, volume, recency, and what do the critical reviews complain about? Check Trustpilot yourself or use our dated verification; recurring complaint themes are the most honest product documentation that exists. Nine: how will this plan handle my dog's next year - the puppy's growth, the senior's slowdown, the diet the vet just ordered? Resize mechanics are future-you's whole experience. Ten: what does my vet think? Thirty seconds at the next visit; a courtesy for the healthy dog, the entire decision for a dog with any medical history. Ten clean answers: subscribe with open eyes. An answer you cannot get: also an answer.",
